    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Chapter 11: The Aggregate Expenditures Model\"\nsubtitle: \"Practice Problems\"\nformat:\n  html: default\n  pdf:\n    documentclass: article\n    pdf-engine: xelatex\n    mainfont: \"Avenir Next\"\n    fontsize: 11pt\n    geometry: margin=0.75in\n    linestretch: 1.1\n    header-includes: |\n      \\usepackage{etoolbox}\n      \\apptocmd{\\maketitle}{\\vspace*{-1.1in}}{}{}\nfilters:\n  - practice-filter.lua\n---\n\n## Warm-Up: Quick Concept and Calculation Checks\n\na. In the aggregate expenditures model, prices are assumed to be sticky in the short run. When\n   total spending changes, does that show up mainly as a change in *real output* or a change in\n   *prices*?\nb. True or False: equilibrium GDP always equals potential (full-employment) GDP.\nc. At a GDP level where planned spending (AE) is *greater* than actual output, what happens to\n   unplanned inventories, and which direction does that push future production?\nd. At a GDP level where planned spending (AE) is *less* than actual output, what happens to\n   unplanned inventories, and which direction does that push future production?\ne. If the MPC in an economy is 0.75, what is the simple multiplier?\nf. A country's aggregate expenditures at full-employment GDP fall $15 billion short of what is\n   needed to reach full-employment GDP. Is this a recessionary or an inflationary expenditure gap?\n\n::: {.solution-only}\n\na. *Real output.* Because prices are sticky in the short run, changes in spending are absorbed by\n   changes in how much firms produce, not by changing prices.\nb. *False.* Equilibrium GDP is simply where planned spending equals output, it can sit above,\n   below, or exactly at potential GDP.\nc. Unplanned inventories *fall* (firms sell more than they planned to produce), signaling firms to\n   *increase* production, pushing GDP *up* toward equilibrium.\nd. Unplanned inventories *rise* (firms sell less than they planned to produce), signaling firms to\n   *decrease* production, pushing GDP *down* toward equilibrium.\ne. $1/(1-0.75) = 1/0.25 = 4$.\nf. A *recessionary expenditure gap* (spending falls short at full employment, producing a negative\n   GDP gap).\n\n:::\n\n## Problem 1: Building the AE Schedule and Finding Equilibrium\n\nA simple, private, closed economy (no government, no trade) has the consumption schedule\n$C = 150 + 0.75 \\times GDP$ (in billions of dollars), and planned investment is constant at\n$I_g = 50$ billion regardless of the level of GDP.\n\n| Real GDP | Consumption (C) | Investment ($I_g$) | Aggregate Expenditures (AE) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 700 | 675 | 50 | ? |\n| 800 | 750 | 50 | ? |\n| 900 | 825 | 50 | ? |\n| 1000 | 900 | 50 | ? |\n\na. Fill in the Aggregate Expenditures column ($AE = C + I_g$) for each row.\nb. At which GDP level in the table does AE equal GDP? This is equilibrium GDP.\nc. At GDP $=1000$, is AE greater than or less than GDP? What does this imply is happening to\n   unplanned inventories and future production at that GDP level?\nd. What is the MPC implied by this consumption schedule, and what is the multiplier?\n\n::: {.solution-only}\n\n::: {#5e069c02 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:420px; width:580px;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/2.7.5/MathJax.js?config=TeX-AMS-MML_SVG\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}</script>                <script>window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};</script>\n        <script charset=\"utf-8\" src=\"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-3.7.0.min.js\" integrity=\"sha256-jvTGqxNp8AGWEcvNLVuKr+8j5dGe9Yw51LQkmDH+IYA=\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>                <div id=\"36e375d9-da2f-46d4-bad4-6bd5267cc54b\" class=\"plotly-graph-div\" style=\"height:100%; width:100%;\"></div>            <script>                window.PL